{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1206 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "aa5a6357d61aabf8f1fa1103caef2ad3d839151288f125d10eac56cd1e99c7ae" :edb/db-before-t 1205 :edb/db-after-t 1206 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534518 50 #inst "2026-10-12T01:42:36.240Z" 13194139534518 17592186045417 true] [17592186082733 1078 #edb/ref 17592186082738 13194139534518 17592186045417 true] [17592186083123 1071 "Given that the continuous random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-1, 4]$, compute $P(0< X < 3)$." 13194139534518 17592186045417 false] [17592186083123 1071 "Given that the continuous random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-1, 4],$ compute $P(0<   X <  3).$" 13194139534518 17592186045417 true] [17592186083123 1074 "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $X$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n![](images/e8/e8a1e7972e4e4a543c2e774894f83ec38755e7b2145a5b301e44906affac9c15.svg)\n\nOur random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-1,4],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n![](images/5c/5ca4ceb0bed3882328dbfa8fb54eb9de948a53d66d9850b8d40781d19f50f4fc.svg)\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0<X<3) & =P(0<X<3) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3-0}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534518 17592186045417 true] [17592186083123 1074 "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $X$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\frac{1}{b - a},\\begin{vmatrix}a \\le x \\le b, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nOur random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-1,4]$, so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{\\frac{1}{5},\\begin{vmatrix}-1 \\le x \\le 4, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(0 < X < 3) &= P(0 < X < 3) \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{3}f(x)dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{5}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{3 - 0}{5} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534518 17592186045417 false] [17592186083129 1071 "Given that the continuous random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $\\left[0, 10\\right]$, compute $P\\left(X \\leq 7\\right)$." 13194139534518 17592186045417 false] [17592186083129 1071 "Given that the continuous random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $\\left[0, 10\\right],$ compute $P\\left(X \\leq 7\\right).$" 13194139534518 17592186045417 true] [17592186083129 1074 "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $X$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n![](images/e7/e76831e0e537ad6fdd429115b56034f54f9071574430c41fb9fe5b4888a72b48.svg)\n\nOur random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $\\left[0, 10\\right],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n![](images/7a/7a7bfa98d0a54a198c0050e763a2f1ac75bca751063b9ea20ec6979163049c6f.svg)\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 7) & =P(0\\le X\\le 7) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{7}\\frac{1}{10}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{7-0}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534518 17592186045417 true] [17592186083129 1074 "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $X$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\frac{1}{b - a},\\begin{vmatrix}a \\le x \\le b, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nOur random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval\n\n$$\n\\left[0, 10\\right]\n$$\n\nso it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{\\frac{1}{10},\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 10, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X \\le 7) &= P(0 \\le X \\le 7) \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{7}f(x)dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{7}\\frac{1}{10}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{7 - 0}{10} \\\\\n&= \\frac{7}{10}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534518 17592186045417 false] [17592186083130 1071 "Given that the continuous random variable $Y$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $\\left[1, 16\\right]$, compute $P\\left(Y \\geq 10\\right)$." 13194139534518 17592186045417 false] [17592186083130 1071 "Given that the continuous random variable $Y$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $\\left[1, 16\\right],$ compute $P\\left(Y  \\geq  10\\right).$" 13194139534518 17592186045417 true] [17592186083130 1074 "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $Y$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n![](images/64/64661e76a430c63a3ba74d43f3d074e43d5c03a1a09c39d714a087d5fb8b5afa.svg)\n\nOur random variable $Y$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $\\left[1, 16\\right],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n![](images/bd/bd2bfc727ccdaeb4d0912640c12fa99900db52102aefe3a3dce46ad2e2cbfef0.svg)\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\ge 10) & =P(10\\le Y\\le 16) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{10}^{16}f(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{10}^{16}\\frac{1}{15}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{16-10}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534518 17592186045417 true] [17592186083130 1074 "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $Y$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\frac{1}{b - a},\\begin{vmatrix}a \\le y \\le b, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nOur random variable $Y$ is uniformly distributed on the interval\n\n$$\n\\left[1, 16\\right]\n$$\n\nso it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{\\frac{1}{15},\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\le y \\le 16, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(Y \\ge 10) &= P(10 \\le Y \\le 16) \\\\\n&= \\int_{10}^{16}f(y)dy \\\\\n&= \\int_{10}^{16}\\frac{1}{15}dy \\\\\n&= \\frac{16 - 10}{15} \\\\\n&= \\frac{6}{15} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534518 17592186045417 false] [17592186083321 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424050 13194139534518 17592186045417 true] [17592186083321 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424054 13194139534518 17592186045417 true] [17592186083327 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424052 13194139534518 17592186045417 true] [17592186083327 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424053 13194139534518 17592186045417 true] [17592186083328 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424051 13194139534518 17592186045417 true] [17592186083469 1050 420.0 13194139534518 17592186045417 true] [17592186083967 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534518 17592186045417 true] [17592186083967 1071 "Given that the continuous random variable $Y$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[- 6,-1]$, compute $P(Y > - 5)$." 13194139534518 17592186045417 false] [17592186083967 1071 "Given that the continuous random variable $Y$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-6,-1],$ compute $P(Y>-5).$" 13194139534518 17592186045417 true] [17592186083967 1074 "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $Y$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le y\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur random variable $Y$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-6,-1],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5},\\, & -6\\le y\\le -1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y>-5) & =P(-5<Y<-1) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-5}^{-1}f(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-5}^{-1}\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{-1-(-5)}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534518 17592186045417 true] [17592186084481 1064 "A continuous random variable $X$ follows a **continuous uniform distribution** on the interval $[a,b]$ if all values of $X$ are equally likely on $[a,b]$.\n\nIntuitively, the familiar statement \"choose a random number between $a$ and $b$\" can be translated as \"observe a value from the continuous uniform distribution on $[a,b]$.\"\n\nThe probability density function for the continuous uniform distribution on an interval $[a,b]$ takes the following form:\n\n$$\n{\\frac{1}{b - a},\\begin{vmatrix}a \\le x \\le b, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the following graph:\n\n![](images/9d/9d077084b025c673533cefc52b7d2e7bfe90e8f903e926a27ce8d1207a0d4e79.png)\n\nNotice that the probability density of the continuous uniform distribution on $[a,b]$ is just $1$ divided by the length of the interval. This ensures that our probability density function has the same value everywhere in the interval and integrates to $1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{a}^{b}\\frac{1}{b - a}dx &= \\left.\\frac{x}{b - a}\\right|_{a}^{b} \\\\\n&= \\frac{b - a}{b - a} \\\\\n&= 1✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen a random variable $X$ follows a continuous uniform distribution on the interval $[a,b]$, we often write\n\n$$\nX\\sim U[a,b]\n$$\n\nand say that \"$X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[a,b]$.\"" 13194139534518 17592186045417 false] [17592186084481 1064 "A continuous random variable $X$ follows a **continuous uniform distribution** on the interval $[a,b]$ if all values of $X$ are equally likely on $[a,b].$\n\nIntuitively, the familiar statement \"choose a random number between $a$ and $b$\" can be translated as \"observe a value from the continuous uniform distribution on $[a,b].$\" \n\nThe probability density function for the continuous uniform distribution on an interval $[a,b]$ takes the following form:\n\n![](images/a2/a247cacd484a99d579d2a30245d7ab495f6d0d116fbf02e2fe8a95fa658df9c6.svg)\n\nThis corresponds to the following graph:\n\n![](images/9d/9d077084b025c673533cefc52b7d2e7bfe90e8f903e926a27ce8d1207a0d4e79.png)\n\nNotice that the probability density of the continuous uniform distribution on $[a,b]$ is just $1$ divided by the length of the interval. This ensures that our probability density function has the same value everywhere in the interval and integrates to $1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{1}{b-a}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\frac{x}{b-a}|}_{a}^{b} \\\\  & =\\frac{b-a}{b-a} \\\\  & =1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen a random variable $X$ follows a continuous uniform distribution on the interval $[a,b],$ we often write\n\n$$\nX\\sim U[a,b]\n$$\n\n \n\nand say that \"$X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[a,b].$\"" 13194139534518 17592186045417 true] [17592186424020 1080 "\\frac{3}{4}" 13194139534518 17592186045417 true] [17592186424020 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534518 17592186045417 true] [17592186424020 1083 #uuid "0e3d93ec-eb09-56cb-a416-5e2e7bec5a82" 13194139534518 17592186045417 true] [17592186424021 1080 "\\frac{3\\pi }{8}" 13194139534518 17592186045417 true] [17592186424021 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534518 17592186045417 true] [17592186424021 1083 #uuid "107d2581-115e-5fcf-a7ba-4b8a4e067792" 13194139534518 17592186045417 true] [17592186424022 1080 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139534518 17592186045417 true] [17592186424022 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534518 17592186045417 true] [17592186424022 1083 #uuid "160a5126-2e24-5c1b-be6a-a06e49dea788" 13194139534518 17592186045417 true] [17592186424023 1080 "\\frac{3}{8}" 13194139534518 17592186045417 true] [17592186424023 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534518 17592186045417 true] [17592186424023 1083 #uuid "19b9c547-b3de-5a3b-af9a-1d4bd21add26" 13194139534518 17592186045417 true] [17592186424024 1080 "0" 13194139534518 17592186045417 true] [17592186424024 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534518 17592186045417 true] [17592186424024 1083 #uuid "19f85468-debe-56c4-9c7b-72ce62714596" 13194139534518 17592186045417 true] [17592186424025 1080 "\\frac{1}{10}" 13194139534518 17592186045417 true] [17592186424025 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534518 17592186045417 true] [17592186424025 1083 #uuid "1acc037d-bce5-53b6-8323-1b252862f034" 13194139534518 17592186045417 true] [17592186424026 1080 "\\frac{3}{7}" 13194139534